  </head>
  <body>
    <SiteHeader />

    <main>
      <section
        class="page-header page-header-split pillar-hero"
        style="--topic: rgba(241,94,74,0.16); --topic-fg: #d8412c;"
      >
        <div class="container">
          <div>
          <div class="breadcrumbs">
            <a href="/">Home</a>
            <span>›</span>
            <a href="/seo">SEO</a>
            <span>›</span>
            <a href="/seo/backlinks">Link Building</a>
            <span>›</span>
            <span>Lifestyle Posting Sites</span>
          </div>

          <span class="card-icon pillar-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-mug-hot" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Link building</p>
          <h1>{title}</h1>
          <p class="pillar-intro">{intro}</p>
          <PageByline />
          </div>
          <div class="posting-hero-figure">
            <img src="/images/posting/lifestyle-posting-sites.png" width="1200" height="675" alt={title} loading="eager" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">"Lifestyle" is not a niche. It is a shelf that holds fashion, beauty, home, food, travel, wellness and parenting, and each of those has its own editors, its own angles, and its own bar.</p>

            <p>Pitch Vogue a home-organisation tip and you will be ignored. Pitch a food site a fashion trend piece and same result. The list of 100 is below. First, work out which slice of it is actually yours.</p>

            <h2 id="subniche">Find your lifestyle sub-niche first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Your sub-niche</th><th>Best fit on this list</th><th>What they run</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Fashion &amp; beauty</td><td>Vogue, Elle, Harper's Bazaar, Byrdie, Who What Wear, The Zoe Report</td><td>Trends, expert how-tos, first-person style essays</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Home &amp; interiors</td><td>Apartment Therapy, Remodelista, Dwell, Elle Decor, Hunker, House Beautiful</td><td>Real projects, before-and-afters, practical guides</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Food &amp; drink</td><td>Serious Eats, Bon Appetit, Food52, The Kitchn, Epicurious, Eater</td><td>Tested recipes, technique, food culture</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Travel</td><td>Afar, Travel + Leisure, Lonely Planet, Matador Network, Nomadic Matt</td><td>First-hand destination pieces, practical trip planning</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Wellness &amp; fitness</td><td>mindbodygreen, Well+Good, Self, Shape, Greatist, Outside</td><td>Evidence-based advice, expert-authored how-tos</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Parenting</td><td>Parents, Scary Mommy, Motherly, Fatherly, MOTHER</td><td>Honest personal essays, practical guides, expert Q&amp;A</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> every site on this list has a high DR. That is not the tie-breaker. The tie-breaker is whether their readers are your customers, and whether the piece you can write fits what that specific editor publishes.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 lifestyle sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; dead and parked sites were removed. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for lifestyle editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Read their last month of coverage.</strong> Lifestyle editors plan around seasons and trends. Pitch into the calendar, not against it.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring a specific angle, not a topic.</strong> Not "fall fashion". Something like "the one autumn wardrobe swap stylists actually make", with the detail to back it.</li>
              <li><strong>Lead with why you.</strong> A credential, a first-hand experience, or original data. Lifestyle runs on voice and authority.</li>
              <li><strong>Match their format.</strong> Personal essay, listicle, how-to, expert Q&amp;A. Pitch the format they use for that topic.</li>
              <li><strong>One clip.</strong> Your strongest piece in a similar style.</li>
              <li><strong>Be honest about any brand tie.</strong> If you are pitching on behalf of a product, say so. Editors decide, but they punish surprises.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor commission this piece from a freelancer with no product to sell? If yes, pitch it. If the article only exists to place a link, it reads that way.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a generic seasonal roundup. Every editor has ten of those already.</li>
              <li>Send the same pitch across sub-niches. A food editor and a fashion editor want completely different things.</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate or exact-match anchor links into the body.</li>
              <li>Submit AI-spun filler with no voice. Lifestyle is a voice-first category and thin content stands out badly.</li>
              <li>Chase the DR 90 titles first. Start with the sites where you can realistically land a yes and build clips.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a handful of placements on sites whose readers are your buyers will do more for your brand and your rankings than a long list of high-DR links in the wrong sub-niche. Use this list to find your handful.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
              {toc.map((item) => (
                <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
